export const body = "<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Workflow overview</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Before blockout</li><li>Blockout</li><li>First-playable</li><li>Final gameplay</li><li>Polishing</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Reading the brief</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Overview</li><li>Understanding the story</li><li>Understanding the setting</li><li>Understanding the gameplay</li><li>Understanding traversal mechanics</li><li>Understanding the metrics</li><li>Understanding the camera</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Reading the concept art</li></ul>\n\n<p>Chapter 06: How Art and Design Collaborate</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>As the title suggests, the primary focus of this chapter is on the relationship between design and art. It is also about the people who work in these two disciplines.</p>\n\n<p>In this chapter, we will first look at the workflow of the designer and the artist to see how they collaborate step by step. Then, we will go into detail of each aspect that they regularly discuss during the process.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a49ccd7f081aceacd557.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a49ccd7f081aceacd557.webp\" width=\"790\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:476px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Workflow overview</h2>\n\n<h3>Before blockout</h3>\n\n<p>In this earliest stage, most of the time is spent on conceptualizing the level. How to tell the story? What kind of gameplay is involved? What is the visual style we should aim for? Together, the designer and the artist will answer these questions to settle on the direction of the level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a0f6f4610daee18db8f7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a0f6f4610daee18db8f7.webp\" width=\"1091\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Blockout</h3>\n\n<p>The blockout stage mainly revolves around gameplay, but the artist still has things to work on. First, they’ll build the proxy models according to the scale and gameplay requirements stated in the brief.</p>\n\n<p>Another aspect to nail during this stage is defining a clear direction of progress for the level. The player should be able to tell where they need to head to.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2de44a2eac074ea06db9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2de44a2eac074ea06db9.webp\" width=\"998\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>First-playable</h3>\n\n<p>Now is the time to flesh out the gameplay of the level. The designer will continue to iterate on the level layout and add gameplay elements (such as enemies). Since the gameplay areas are still provisional, the level artist’s focus will be on the non-playable areas.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/da4ea17d49a4257d1634.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/da4ea17d49a4257d1634.webp\" width=\"1025\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Final gameplay</h3>\n\n<p>Once the gameplay is finalized, the level designer will stop iterating on the level. That means, the level artist can jump in and start working on the playable areas.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f2c0022edb1f4bb955c7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f2c0022edb1f4bb955c7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"990\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Polishing</h3>\n\n<p>The final stretch of the level-making process is dedicated to optimization and upgrading the visuals.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e0752124a7024fe01c50.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e0752124a7024fe01c50.webp\" width=\"1147\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> — Workflow overview</strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Blockout</p></td><td><p>First texture pass</p></td><td><p>Paintover feedback</p></td><td><p>Final texture pass</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7d6478d4f829d0fd02f4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7d6478d4f829d0fd02f4.webp\" width=\"313\" height=\"177\" style=\"--source-width:108px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/50a17d276609a9dd75a0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/50a17d276609a9dd75a0.webp\" width=\"313\" height=\"177\" style=\"--source-width:147px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/639fc4f9a9f988be347a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/639fc4f9a9f988be347a.webp\" width=\"315\" height=\"150\" style=\"--source-width:147px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7de6f62bb9dadb1ced98.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7de6f62bb9dadb1ced98.webp\" width=\"313\" height=\"177\" style=\"--source-width:147px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong><strong> — </strong><strong>Playtesting process and best practices</strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/89471951045450811d78.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/89471951045450811d78.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"449\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Reading the brief</h2>\n\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n\n<p>A level brief is a document detailing different aspects of a level, such as:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>The story</strong></li><li><strong>The location</strong></li><li><strong>The intended emotion</strong></li><li><strong>Time of day</strong></li><li><strong>Available traversal mechanics</strong></li><li><strong>Etc.</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p>It’s recommended that you read everything, even things outside your scope of work, to have a holistic understanding of the level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0c19ae5599c799efaaf4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0c19ae5599c799efaaf4.webp\" width=\"880\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding the story</h3>\n\n<p>The story should be a priority learning point for your level.</p>\n\n<ul><li>Where am I in the story?</li><li>Where does this level take place?</li><li>What will happen in this level?</li><li>What is the core emotion?</li><li>Does the player learn any new mechanic?</li><li>If yes, how can we teach it?</li><li>Etc.</li></ul>\n\n<p>As an example, every time the player acquires a new ability in<em> Jedi Fallen Order</em>, they will enter a flashback sequence in which the main character recalls his first time learning the ability.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a77aecf9088e0f0d7143.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a77aecf9088e0f0d7143.webp\" width=\"1053\" height=\"592\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d013dc83fc2f56d2dcc5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d013dc83fc2f56d2dcc5.webp\" width=\"1062\" height=\"597\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Understanding the story</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>The Last of Us 2</em> opens with a snowfight that doubles as a tutorial for the controls.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/08cf6568bcadba84f84e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/08cf6568bcadba84f84e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"303\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The 4th mission in <em>Dishonored 2</em> takes place in the house of a genius inventor. Obsessed with mechanical creation, the inventor built his place of residence with many moving parts, a house made of gears and cogs. The Clockwork Mansion.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a3ea9144355f7a8db3b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a3ea9144355f7a8db3b.webp\" width=\"591\" height=\"341\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f79b405fa1abbb99f148.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f79b405fa1abbb99f148.webp\" width=\"604\" height=\"341\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA</strong><strong> – Choreography</strong></p>\n\n<p>Other than props, the player character will likely interact with non-playable characters (NPCs) to varying degrees. The act of scripting NPcs to behave beyond their regular boundaries is called “choreography.”</p>\n\n<p>There is a variety of applications for choreography. Sometimes, those scripted interactions are used for guidance; other times, they are used for jumpscares. The most important thing regarding choreography for level artists is ensuring all scripted events are triggered as intended.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe23d4863d9fe7c7d095.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe23d4863d9fe7c7d095.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"622\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/aa151becd83c63f132f5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/aa151becd83c63f132f5.webp\" width=\"709\" height=\"397\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding the setting</h3>\n\n<p>Chapter 13 in <em>Uncharted 4</em> has a section that takes place in a cave. The enclosed space, coupled with the dark interior, induces a claustrophobic feel, which is relieved by the eventual lead-up back to the ground.</p>\n\n<p>Without that claustrophobic design, exiting the cave wouldn’t feel as refreshing.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/348cf593ccf8c72ce0eb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/348cf593ccf8c72ce0eb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"644\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e3aadeeab503ba1bfcd0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e3aadeeab503ba1bfcd0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Understanding the setting</strong></p>\n\n<p>Each location in <em>Resident Evil Village </em>has a distinct theme. The first level is a Gothic-inspired castle, and the second level is a doll house.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0c479f3da8b8cad91c79.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0c479f3da8b8cad91c79.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The Pittsburg streets in <em>The Last of Us</em> is flooded, so some parts of the environment are submerged.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9e9c8c3680ccb882f286.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9e9c8c3680ccb882f286.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding the gameplay</h3>\n\n<p>Gameplay varies from game to game, and nowadays AAA games tend to have multiple types of gameplay in summation. And within each of these games, the gameplay varies from level to level. Therefore, you have to understand what types of gameplay your level has to do the art accordingly.</p>\n\n<p>For example, the 5th mission in <em>Titanfall 2</em> gives the player a time-travel device. Just by a button press, they can switch between the present and the future. This level basically layers two environments on top of each other. Although the layout remains unchanged across, the props are drastically different.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1bf63cb1717404b8ed4d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1bf63cb1717404b8ed4d.webp\" width=\"720\" height=\"405\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b576c21c612343666b04.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b576c21c612343666b04.webp\" width=\"720\" height=\"405\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Understanding the gameplay</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e15afd63cab31df4b7bf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e15afd63cab31df4b7bf.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>In this gameplay demo of <em>Path of Exile 2</em>, the boss has a slam attack that cause rocks to fall from above to damage the player. As he destroys the ceiling, light pools into the arena, and the player can stay within the lit region to avoid further debris falling.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2a6cb4f5e5c0d8f99bcc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2a6cb4f5e5c0d8f99bcc.webp\" width=\"635\" height=\"354\" style=\"--source-width:125px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4ba5b67af4f26092a2ae.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4ba5b67af4f26092a2ae.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding traversal mechanics</h3>\n\n<p>Traversal mechanics are movement abilities of the player character. As a level artist, it’s important that you understand how the player moves in the environment, so that you can employ a consistent visual language.</p>\n\n<p>For example, in <em>Shadow of the Tomb Raider</em>, Lara Croft can scale certain walls using her pickaxes. These walls, as a result, have to apply a distinct texture to tell the player that they are climable.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ad5b2a4ec62e90472c97.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ad5b2a4ec62e90472c97.webp\" width=\"767\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:459px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Understanding the traversal mechanics</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3d675be31ccf438727f8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3d675be31ccf438727f8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p>As for static props that nudge the player to perform an action—crawling or squeezing through, for example—remember to follow the design metrics so that the player won’t have to guess whether they can do something or not.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9491657fd8bf788f1467.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9491657fd8bf788f1467.webp\" width=\"681\" height=\"382\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fcf644a53df78b027682.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fcf644a53df78b027682.webp\" width=\"681\" height=\"382\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding the metrics</h3>\n\n<p>Speaking of metrics, here is a reminder from Chapter 2. Other than metrics for traversal abilities, here are some numbers you should keep in mind:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Character height</strong></li><li><strong>Average wall height, width, depth and length</strong></li><li><strong>Door and doorway height and width</strong></li><li><strong>Stair step height and depth</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p>A common way to stay close to the metrics is using grid-based content.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c6ef26807c52bd606e8d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c6ef26807c52bd606e8d.webp\" width=\"1000\" height=\"750\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/db547f3922d7986acef3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/db547f3922d7986acef3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"719\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – Understanding the metrics.</strong></p>\n\n<p>In <em>Resident Evil 4</em>, the player can jump over obstacles that are below the character’s chest level. As you can see in the left picture, there is a JUMP OVER prompt for the barricade. The metal panels in the right picture, however, don’t allow the player to jump over despite having the same height as the blocker in the left picture.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/89b18e6244673d1cad28.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/89b18e6244673d1cad28.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><br>In <em>God of War (2018)</em>, Kratos is capable of vaulting over objects that are 3 meters tall. But he cannot vault over the railing in the bottom picture, which can be confusing to the player. This situation would be less questionable if the game didn’t place an item box below to attract the player’s attention.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8f85bb9d1b81c101d23a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8f85bb9d1b81c101d23a.webp\" width=\"878\" height=\"356\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8d708354bd717b96f01a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8d708354bd717b96f01a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding the camera</h3>\n\n<p>We briefly talked about camera systems in chapter 2. The core idea of that sub-section was “The closer the player can view an object, the more detailed it should be.” In this context, <em>more detailed</em> means greater asset fidelity, which includes geometry and texel density.</p>\n\n<p>Texel density is the texture resolution on a mesh. Ideally, nearby objects should be high resolution, and object far way lower resolution. And dictating this near-far relationship is the camera system.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/989de8a2eeadf15946c4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/989de8a2eeadf15946c4.webp\" width=\"696\" height=\"934\" style=\"--source-width:445px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP </strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Understanding the camera</strong></p>\n\n<p>Different camera systems have different zoom levels. But generally, we can focus on these 3 categories:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>First-person</strong>: requires the most texel density because things can be viewed upclose; more work goes into hands, guns, pickup items, etc.</li><li><strong>Third-person</strong>: more work goes into the player character model and things they can get close to</li><li><strong>Top-down</strong>: everything is viewed from far above, so the environment doesn’t need high texel density</li></ul>\n\n<p>Knowing the view distance for objects in your scene will help prevent putting low-res textures in front of the player.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d16a695cf48ef3a9390a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d16a695cf48ef3a9390a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"678\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d207b54d7b1132b0ef87.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d207b54d7b1132b0ef87.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:490px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Reading the concept art</h2>\n\n<p>The concept art is going to frame the scene, giving us an early look at the environment and its components. Importantly, it should provide us with a story.</p>\n\n<p>As an example, these two concepts tell us that:</p>\n\n<ul><li>The criminal is into boxing with all the boxing images on the wall</li><li>The criminal is addicted to pornography: the wallpaper, the magazines, and the TV are all showing sexual content</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2b84fd3927454e1a41d8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2b84fd3927454e1a41d8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"511\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 41\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0f3330eb08dfbb4d772d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0f3330eb08dfbb4d772d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"500\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 42\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Another kind of information to extract from concepts is how modular the environment is. The opposite examples from <em>Gears 5</em> feature many structural components, which would be perfect candidates for modules.</p>\n\n<p>In regard to gameplay, the concepts here tell us what could be used for cover in this context. With that information, we can put objects used for cover in a separate list with correct metrics for later modeling.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/93b4657b82e46117f3da.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/93b4657b82e46117f3da.webp\" width=\"565\" height=\"283\" style=\"--source-width:565px\" alt=\"Illustration 43\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/62371ae89b8a5dcc95ea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/62371ae89b8a5dcc95ea.webp\" width=\"565\" height=\"312\" style=\"--source-width:565px\" alt=\"Illustration 44\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Reading the concept art</strong></p>\n\n<p>There are a lot of modules to be made out of these rock structures.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ae9f8bdf07cf638d2b3a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ae9f8bdf07cf638d2b3a.webp\" width=\"657\" height=\"281\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 45\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3446bf5375460cef06b2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3446bf5375460cef06b2.webp\" width=\"657\" height=\"280\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 46\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PROTIP</strong><strong> – Case study 1</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/988216d895004a70efee.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/988216d895004a70efee.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"577\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 47\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p>One of the first things we see in this concept is the cloudy gray sky. This gives us an idea for the time of day, and specifically what kind of ambient lighting we can employ.</p>\n\n<p>On the ground, we can see a lot of ashes an debris. And because it’s a battlefield, we can assume there are also bullet shells.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e9ba7301d6d468671388.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e9ba7301d6d468671388.webp\" width=\"638\" height=\"481\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 48\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ef149f243331560b1582.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ef149f243331560b1582.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"577\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 49\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/06fbd07ede7ed40e17e9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/06fbd07ede7ed40e17e9.webp\" width=\"596\" height=\"336\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 50\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p>The building here is showing the internal structure of metal beams. Realistically, a metal formwork would only work with concrete, but based on the color, we can assume bricks are involved in this case. So, the best thing to do is asking the concept artist what he or she is going for.</p>\n\n<p>Toward the bottom right, we see some damaged metal plates. What we need to extract here is what type of bullets was used so that we can create appropriate bullet holes.</p>\n\n<p>As for the skeleton, we will need to know how long it’s been there, and what the cause of death was.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/38a864cf48cb33308096.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/38a864cf48cb33308096.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"801\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 51\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Case study 2</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/12bc81322efea7b7773f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/12bc81322efea7b7773f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 52\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p>The lighting is perhaps the most prominent aspect of this concept.</p>\n\n<p>1.First, we have only natural light in here</p>\n\n<p>2.Second, we know which direction the light is pooling in thanks to the broken windows</p>\n\n<p>3.Third, we can tell this is nighttime thanks to the blue-tinged ambience</p>\n\n<p>Another interesting detail to pick out is the particles floating in the air. If you were to handle this level, you might want to check with the VFX team to see how they approach it. Particle effects are performance intensive, so be careful with them.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4e9f094a14a2bd87100f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4e9f094a14a2bd87100f.webp\" width=\"723\" height=\"409\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 53\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>With the lighting setup figured out, we can start looking at the objects. In this scene, the ground is the most complex element, featuring a lot of debris of wood and concrete (based on construction materials). To the far left, there is pile of broken timber and bricks. If you look at the ceiling, you can see splinters up there (which also allows light to stream in).</p>\n\n<p>Though the object count is low, the props look unique enough to stir curiosity. Therefore, you may need to check whether they are static or dynamic.</p>\n\n<p>Besides, you may want to request detailed asset concepts (or references) for some objects that are obscured in shadow, such the discs on the floating shelf on the left.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7f630eb2fc54bb4756b7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7f630eb2fc54bb4756b7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"650\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 54\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
